Add a plain net8.0 target framework to EdiabasLib so it builds on macOS/Linux.

Upstream targets only net*-windows and net481. Nothing in the OBD serial path
actually needs Windows: EdInterfaceObd drives System.IO.Ports.SerialPort, which
is cross-platform on .NET 8+. The Windows-only surface is confined to
Ftd2xx.cs (P/Invoke into the native ftd2xx library, which also ships for macOS)
and Bluetooth, neither of which is on the K+DCAN cable path this app uses.

Assembly signing is disabled because EdiabasLib.snk is not needed for a local
reference build and strong-name signing adds nothing here.

--- a/EdiabasLib/EdiabasLib/EdiabasLib.csproj
+++ b/EdiabasLib/EdiabasLib/EdiabasLib.csproj
@@
-    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0;net8.0-windows10.0.26100.0;net481;$(_AndroidTargets)</TargetFrameworks>
+    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0;net8.0-windows10.0.26100.0;net481;net8.0;$(_AndroidTargets)</TargetFrameworks>
@@
-    <SignAssembly>true</SignAssembly>
+    <SignAssembly Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' != 'net8.0'">true</SignAssembly>
@@
-  <ItemGroup Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('windows'))">
+  <ItemGroup Condition="$(TargetFramework.Contains('windows')) or '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
       <PackageReference Include="InTheHand.BluetoothLE" Version="4.0.45" GeneratePathProperty="true" />
       <PackageReference Include="System.IO.Ports" Version="10.0.12" GeneratePathProperty="true" />
   </ItemGroup>
